  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nAnother night, another round of severe thunderstorms and heavy \nrain. The good news is that the upper level trough and surface \nconvergence that has lead to multiple nights of severe weather are\nfinally moving to the east, but not before we could see one more \nround of strong thunderstorms on Monday. The main threat for these\nstrong thunderstorms will be in Hudspeth County. Most of the \nregion will stay rain free on Monday. High temperatures on Monday \nwill run a few degrees below average.\n\nFor Tuesday and Wednesday we will see drier air move into the \narea on a zonal (west to east) flow aloft. We look to stay dry \nboth days with high temperatures climbing a few degrees above \naverage by Wednesday. Wednesday afternoon our upper level flow \nwill begin to turn more to the southeast as our next upper level \ntrough approaches the west coast. Our precipitable water values \n(PW's) will drop below 0.5 for most of the area on Tuesday and \nWednesday. But then on Thursday, we will see another surge of \nmoisture from the south and southeast and we will see PW's jump to\naround 0.80 to 0.90 by Thursday. With the moisture in place and \nthe next upper level trough approaching the area will be enough to\ngive us a chance for thunderstorms Thursday afternoon and \nevening. On Friday the main energy from the upper level trough \nwill sweep across New Mexico and that will give us a good chance \nfor thunderstorms. With the well above average PW's we could see \nsome heavy rain makers which can lead to isolated flash flooding. \nHigh temperatures for both Thursday and Friday will run near \nseasonal averages. Right now next Saturday and Sunday look dry, \nwith high temperatures near average.",
